course: dictionary
lesson: 4104
title: One word, two meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. To find the right one, read the words around the word, because the context leaves clues. Then swap each meaning into the sentence and see which one makes sense.
explain: «When two people change the same line of code, their work clashes and must be merged carefully, and tools like Git help teams combine everyone's changes.» Here Git is a computer tool, a program that keeps track of changes. The capital G shows it is a name.
explain: In some British and Irish speech, a git is a rude insult for a person who is annoying or unpleasant. It hurts feelings, so we never use it in class or with friends. When you see a word with more than one meaning, the sentence will tell you which one the writer chose.
fact: A git is a rude word, used in British and Irish slang, for an annoying, stupid or unpleasant person.
fact: Git, with a capital G, is the name of a computer program that programmers use to save versions of their code.
fact: «Saving versions means you can go back if something breaks.» Programmers use Git to keep every version of their work.
fact: When two people change the same part of the code, the changes must be merged, and Git helps teams combine their work.
fact: A capital letter in the middle of a sentence is a clue that a word is a name, such as Git.
exercise: «Tools like Git help teams combine everyone's changes.» What is Git in this sentence? | A kind of football team | A type of bird | A rude word for a person | *A computer tool for keeping track of changes in code
exercise: «The changes must be merged.» What does merged mean here? | Painted in bright colours | Read out loud | *Combined or joined together | Deleted completely
exercise: Lily shouts, «You stupid git!» What does this show? | *It is a rude insult that hurts feelings | It means the person has won a prize | It is a friendly compliment | It is a name for a computer program
test: «When two people change the same line of code, their work clashes.» What does clashes mean here? | *Does not match, so it must be fixed | Is finished and put away | Makes a loud noise like cymbals | Goes very fast
test: «Saving versions means you can go back if something breaks.» Why would a programmer save versions? | So the code gets shorter by itself | So they can forget their work | So the computer gets hot | *So they can go back to an earlier version
exam: Why is there a capital G in Git? | It is a mistake with no reason | It shows the word is a rude insult | *It shows Git is a name, a computer tool, not an ordinary word | It shows the word is very loud
